# The oracle images, pinned.
#
# One line per oracle, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee. A stock image is pinned by digest and
# the digest is the whole guarantee. An image **built here** cannot be: two
# builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is pinned
# instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, pip packages by
# exact version, Debian's font packages by full apt version - and the run-time
# version check is the real guarantee, which is why oracle_env.check_pin() runs
# in *both* modes in this library. `unicode` relaxed that check in host mode
# because every image it names is a stock one; here there is no stock image to
# name, so the relaxation would leave nothing checking anything.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say it is when asked. Raising a
# pin is deliberate: a new fontTools is new answers, and a new answer that
# disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   podman build -t ghoti-font-oracle-fonttools:4.66.0 \
#       -f tools/oracle/containers/fonttools/Containerfile \
#       tools/oracle/containers/fonttools
# `make oracle-build` does exactly that.

# fontTools, and the real fonts. Both halves are deliberate: this is the
# reference for every table's fields (ttx_diff) and for every codepoint's glyph
# (cmap_diff), *and* the image the fixture generator will run in, so that a
# fixture's bytes depend on a pinned fontTools rather than on whatever the host
# has (design.md section 14.7).
fonttools	localhost/ghoti-font-oracle-fonttools:4.66.0	fontTools 4.66.0 Pillow 12.3.0	Every sfnt table's fields, every cmap's mapping, EBLC/EBDT strikes glyph by glyph, PCF and BDF bitmaps through Pillow, and the corpus of real fonts - 507 sfnts selected by magic rather than by name, including the whole Debian population of embedded bitmap strikes, which is thirty-three, sixteen variable TrueType fonts from four producers (Inter, Cascadia Code, Vazirmatn, Sahel) for fvar, avar, gvar and cvar, and the one CFF2 variable font (Cantarell) for blend

# FreeType, built here, driver compiled inside the image against FreeType alone.
#
# What it answers today is the bitmap tables, and the three questions it answers
# were each unanswerable before it: `mona.ttf`'s pixels, which fontTools cannot
# reach at all; a composite's **placement**, which the specification does not
# state and which this library took from `ttsbit.c`, so the rule and its only
# witness were one source read once; and the set of glyphs a strike carries, over
# a population where fontTools and this library disagreed about 13,249 of
# Konatu.ttf's glyphs and FreeType broke the tie.
#
# The outline and coverage differentials (phase 1) are the other half of this
# image's eventual job and are not written. The driver is one program per
# question, so that is a new program beside `freetype-strikes`, not a change to it.
#
# This image carries **no fonts**: corpus.py materialises the fonttools image's
# corpus under build/oracle/corpus, which is inside the tree every oracle
# container mounts read-only, so both references read the same bytes on the host.
freetype	localhost/ghoti-font-oracle-freetype:2.14.3	FreeType 2.14.3	EBLC/EBDT strikes glyph by glyph - the second reading of every composite's pixels, the only reading of mona.ttf besides this library's, and the only reading of a strike's own sbitLineMetrics (for a bitmap-only sfnt, which is the one shape where FreeType reports them)

# Not here yet, and each is a phase rather than an afternoon:
#   harfbuzz   hb-shape, already a batch tool (phase 3)
#   pango      pango-view, for paragraph layout (phase 3b)
#   xfonts     bdftopcf and psftools: a *second* reading of PCF, and the only
#              reading of PSF and `.hex` there is. Pillow covers PCF's bitmaps
#              and BDF outright, so what is still missing is PSF, `.hex`, and an
#              independent reading of PCF's encodings table - see bitmap_diff.py
#
# `mona.ttf` was the first thing the freetype image was pointed at, and it read
# all three strikes: 346,780 lines of protocol, of which 28 differ and none is a
# pixel. What remains unread by any second reader is nothing in this population -
# which is a sentence that could not be written before that image existed.
